/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  /* Small, non-growing, hard-edged, and confined to the top-left square by the
     same two borders the corpus map uses. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type SNOW
    base_size 3
    land_percent 0
    right_border 60
    bottom_border 60
    border_fuzziness 0
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_51 - is the cross-shaped origin region absolute, or relative to what the
borders leave?
Feeds: preview-design Sec.6.1, lands.ts, Sec.15 item 26

QUESTION
An earlier batch measured where a neutral land's origin is allowed to fall and
found a cross-shaped region: a candidate is rejected only when it is far from
the centre on BOTH axes at once, so the four corners are excluded and the four
arms are not. Sec.6.1 applies that unconditionally.

Every land measured for that rule had NO borders. This test asks what happens
when borders have already confined the land somewhere the cross does not like.

WHY IT IS NOT ACADEMIC
A corpus map carries islands with a right and a bottom border of 60, which on a
200 map allows the origin anywhere in the top-left square of side 80. The cross
rejects anything more than about 34 tiles from the centre on both axes at once.
Intersect the two and what is left is an L-shaped sliver hugging the inner edges
of the border box: the islands always crowd toward the middle of the map and
never reach the outer corner. That is visible on the render and it is what a
reader notices and reports as a bug.

Nothing is implemented wrongly. The question is whether the engine's restriction
is absolute in map coordinates, as modelled, or relative to the region the
borders leave - in which case a bordered land scatters evenly across its box and
the corpus map's islands should reach the corner.

METHOD
One land, small, with the same two borders as the corpus map that raised this,
and a terrain nothing else on the map uses so the patch centroid IS the origin.
Twenty generations. Plot the twenty centroids.

  clustered along an L, hugging the inner edges of the box, corner empty
      -> ABSOLUTE. The current model is right, the corpus map's islands are
         faithful, and the answer to the reader is that the engine does this.

  scattered evenly over the whole box, corner as populated as anywhere else
      -> RELATIVE. Sec.6.1 needs the cross re-expressed against the allowed
         region rather than the map, and every bordered land on every map moves.

  something else - a band, a centre bias, an even scatter with a soft corner
      -> record the twenty points and fit nothing yet. Two models is the number
         of models anyone has thought of, not the number that exist, and this
         project has already spent several sittings on questions where the true
         answer was a third shape.

Twenty runs is the cost and it is the point. One origin is a sample of a
distribution and this whole question is about the shape of that distribution -
the same mistake would be to run three and see a pattern.

The land is tiny and does not grow, so each generation costs almost nothing and
the patch is unambiguous. Border fuzziness is off so the box edges are hard.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count. TWENTY RUNS.

Regenerate between saves and check the centroid actually moves. An earlier batch
exported the same generated map three times without noticing: the file hashes
differed, because a scenario embeds its own filename, while the probe output was
identical. Twenty identical centroids would be that mistake, not a result.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches SNOW
  Take the patch centroid from each of the twenty exports and plot them. The
  shape of the scatter is the whole reading; no single export says anything.
*/
